        "The accounting equation states that assets equal capital plus liabilities, and every transaction leaves it balanced. Buying a delivery van for N4,500,000 cash swaps one asset for another; buying it on credit raises both assets and liabilities by the same amount; paying a creditor reduces both. Profit earned increases capital, drawings reduce it. This dual aspect is the mechanical foundation of {S}, and the ledger is built to enforce it automatically.",
        "Definitions drive classification. Assets are resources controlled by the entity from which future benefits are expected; liabilities are present obligations requiring outflows; capital is the owner's residual claim. Income increases equity other than owner contributions, expenses decrease it other than drawings. A three-year prepaid rent, a bank overdraft and unpaid salaries each find their home through these tests. Precise definitions matter in {S} because misclassification distorts both the trial balance and the final accounts.",
        "Each ledger account is a T with a debit side and a credit side, and the rules are fixed: assets and expenses increase on the debit; liabilities, capital and income increase on the credit. Decreases take the opposite side. The mnemonic is debit what comes in, credit what goes out, applied to value rather than cash. Every entry in {S} needs at least one debit and one credit of equal size, which is why the system self-checks.",
        "Recording starts with source documents: the invoice, receipt, credit note, cheque counterfoil and pay voucher. The book of prime entry, or journal, records the transaction in date order with narration, showing the accounts to be debited and credited before posting. Sales day book lists credit sales invoices; purchases day book lists credit purchases; the journal proper handles openings, corrections and unusual entries. Document trail discipline is what auditors later test in {S}, so filing matters as much as posting.",
