        "Splitting capital and revenue expenditure is a classic trial-balance trap. Buying a generator is capital expenditure, debited to an asset and depreciated over years; repairing it is revenue expenditure, expensed immediately. Capitalising a repair overstates profit and assets; expensing a purchase understates them. CAMA 2020 and IFRS both demand the correct split, and receipts are symmetrical: sale of old vans is capital income, while discount received is revenue. The distinction threads through every section of {S}.",
